  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/...\nSaturday max temperatures will trend ~20 degrees cooler than \nFriday's max temps in the wake of a strong cold front. \nPrecipitation will spread into the area Saturday, with highest \nchances expected across the West-Central/Central Idaho and Boise\nMountains. Snow levels will lower from northwest to southeast \nthroughout the day, allowing for light snow accumulations in \nwest-central Idaho above 7500 feet MSL by Saturday night. Cold \nair advection from the northwest will continue into Sunday, \nresulting in max temperatures lowering by an additional 5-10 \ndegrees. High elevation light snow will continue over the West- \nCentral/Central Idaho mountains through Sunday. Meanwhile, \nhurricane remnants originating from the eastern Pacific will \ncarry a significant bout of moisture northeastward through the \nwestern U.S. The bulk of the precipitation is expected to reach \nthe Northern Rockies by mid-Sunday and continue into Monday, \nwith the western edge of the precipitation clipping southwest \nIdaho. While the exact path of moisture is still a bit \nuncertain, higher confidence for notable precipitation is \ncurrently anticipated for the SW Idaho Highlands, Magic Valley, \nCamas Prairie, and the Central Idaho Mountains, with lower \nchances farther west and north. Precipitation from this system \nwill taper Monday night as both the parent trough and tropical \nmoisture exit the area. Some guidance members are indicating a \nsecondary, colder trough could usher into E Oregon and SW Idaho \nlate Tuesday or Wednesday, while other members suggest a slight \nblocking pattern that would result in drier, warmer \nconditions.",
